# Summary
Introduced: the commit adds PG peering metadata read routes and switches read paths from the active primary to metadata_pg_read_node. The certification logic accepts higher-index metadata proofs without verifying that they are the committed proof or a cryptographically/logically proven descendant of it.
During PG peering, the new read route selection accepts any acting-set node whose observed metadata proof passes validate_peering_metadata_proof_floor. That ultimately relies on metadata_proof_satisfies_active_floor, which returns true if observed.applied_log_index is greater than the floor, without checking applied_log_hash or state_digest. The route then authorizes normal object reads through metadata_pg_read_node and open_object_read_metadata_route. A replica with a higher but divergent metadata log can therefore be used as the authority for bucket/object ACLs, delete markers, object existence, tags, and listings during peering. In security terms, a stale/divergent public ACL or missing delete/private transition can be used to serve object data or listings that should no longer be readable.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [063bdc4a029e92c6f67f1fc897ee0c51999723f8](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/063bdc4a029e92c6f67f1fc897ee0c51999723f8) (`Require exact proofs for peering metadata reads`).

Peering read certification is now separate from the broader recovery-progress validator. `peering_metadata_read_route_for_snapshot` issues a `PgMetadataReadRoute` only when the observed complete metadata proof exactly equals the committed Peering floor, or exactly equals the imported metadata proof carried by the active transfer certificate. An arbitrary higher log index, including one paired with a divergent log hash or state digest, no longer grants read authority. Existing health, current-epoch, `Peering`, and empty-pending-command requirements remain in force.

The provenance-aware progression predicate remains available to Peering completion and metadata migration/recovery, where later progress has separate recovery semantics. It is no longer used as a serving-read certification predicate. This preserves recovery behavior without treating an unproven descendant tuple as committed authorization state. Progress beyond the committed proof now fails closed for metadata reads until the control plane durably commits or otherwise certifies it.

Regression coverage proves that the exact committed floor remains readable, higher divergent and below-floor proofs are rejected, pending commands suppress certification, and imported transfers accept only their exact imported proof rather than either the source floor or another proof. The storage invariant guide and multihost transition plan now document this strict boundary. The complete workspace suite passed with 7,978 tests, and `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` passed.

